      "rationale": "[Literature audit 2026-11-05 (deliverome-analysis PR 236), verdict confirmed_exact: position confirmed at this residue. validation_level surface_only -> detected_only (paper reports surface: not_reported, function: not_reported). Paper (PMID 38985877): \"containing superfolder GFP1-10 (44967, Addgene) flanked by G-S linkers after AJAP1-R170\". Audit note: Mouse R170 (FDFRGGRPTTETEF) aligns to human R170 (FDSRGSRPTTETEF) with zero offset. GN-AJAP1 was used for split-GFP complementation with GC-GB1a (trans-synaptic assay), which implies extracellular exposure but is not a conventional surface-display measurement; function of the tagged protein not separately shown. Large FP insert, not an epitope tag.] The ledger explicitly names R170 as the junction for a large sfGFP1-10 domain insertion (flanked by G-S linkers) in the extracellular domain of full-length, membrane-anchored AJAP1. Python lookup confirms human position 170 = R, matching the paper exactly. The site is 127 residues from the mature N-terminus and 113 residues from the TM boundary. If the extracellular domain tolerates a ~230 aa sfGFP1-10 insertion here, a ~15\u201323 aa ALFA tag with GS linkers is sterically far less demanding and is well-supported at this junction. Full-length mouse AJAP1 (membrane-anchored, pCI-AJAP1-mCherry backbone) with superfolder GFP1-10 flanked by G-S linkers inserted after R170 in the extracellular domain, generating GN-AJAP1-mCherry used for split-GFP surface display detection. Human canonical position 170 = R, an exact match to the paper's stated R170; no numbering repair needed.",
